Then, we use $\sin^2 x+\cos^2 x=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:

$$
\begin{aligned}
& 6(\cos^{2} x+\sin^{2} x)-10 \\ &= -4
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, we conclude that

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \cos x(6\cos x-10\frac{1}{\cos x}) \\ &\quad {}+6\sin^{2} x \\ &= -4.
\end{aligned}
$$

