Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. The outer product and the exponential chain rule both matter. First differentiate the exponential input:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{d}{dx}\left(- x y\right)&=- \frac{dy}{dx} x - y\\
\frac{d}{dx}\left(e^{- x y}\right)&=\left(- \frac{dy}{dx} x - y\right) e^{- x y}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$, then collect the derivative terms:

$$
\begin{aligned}
&\frac{d}{dx}\left(x^{2} + 2 y e^{- x y}\right)=\frac{d}{dx}\left(1 + 2 e\right)\\
&2 \frac{dy}{dx} e^{- x y} + 2 x + 2 y \left(- \frac{dy}{dx} x - y\right) e^{- x y}=0\\
&\left(- 2 x y e^{- x y} + 2 e^{- x y}\right)\frac{dy}{dx}=-\left(2 x - 2 y^{2} e^{- x y}\right)\\
&\frac{dy}{dx}=-\frac{2 x - 2 y^{2} e^{- x y}}{- 2 x y e^{- x y} + 2 e^{- x y}}
\end{aligned}
$$

At $(-1,1)$, the coefficient of $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ is $4 e\ne 0$. Substitute the point to obtain

$$
\begin{aligned}
m&=\left.\frac{dy}{dx}\right|_{(-1,1)}\\
&=-\frac{- 2 e - 2}{4 e}\\
&=\frac{1 + e}{2 e}.
\end{aligned}
$$

