Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. The outer product and the exponential chain rule both matter. First differentiate the exponential input:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{d}{dx}\left(- x y\right)&=- \frac{dy}{dx} x - y\\
\frac{d}{dx}\left(e^{- x y}\right)&=\left(- \frac{dy}{dx} x - y\right) e^{- x y}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$, then collect the derivative terms:

$$
\begin{aligned}
&\frac{d}{dx}\left(- 2 x e^{- x y} + 2 y^{2}\right)=\frac{d}{dx}\left(\frac{2}{e} + 2\right)\\
&4 \frac{dy}{dx} y - 2 x \left(- \frac{dy}{dx} x - y\right) e^{- x y} - 2 e^{- x y}=0\\
&\left(2 x^{2} e^{- x y} + 4 y\right)\frac{dy}{dx}=-\left(2 x y e^{- x y} - 2 e^{- x y}\right)\\
&\frac{dy}{dx}=-\frac{2 x y e^{- x y} - 2 e^{- x y}}{2 x^{2} e^{- x y} + 4 y}
\end{aligned}
$$

At $(-1,-1)$, the coefficient of $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ is $-4 + \frac{2}{e}\ne 0$. Substitute the point to obtain

$$
\begin{aligned}
m&=\left.\frac{dy}{dx}\right|_{(-1,-1)}\\
&=-\frac{0}{-4 + \frac{2}{e}}\\
&=0.
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a function $y(x)$. The chain rule gives $\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}x}(3 \tan{\left(3 x \right)})=9 \sec^{2}{\left(3 x \right)}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}x}\left(y^{2} + 2 y\right)&=\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}x}\left(3 \tan{\left(3 x \right)}\right)\\
2 y\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}+2\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}&=9 \sec^{2}{\left(3 x \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Collect the terms containing $\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}$, factor, and solve:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left(2 y + 2\right)\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}&=9 \sec^{2}{\left(3 x \right)}\\
\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}&=\frac{9 \sec^{2}{\left(3 x \right)}}{2 y + 2}
\end{aligned}
$$

This formula applies on real branches where $2 y + 2\ne 0$.

Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a function $y(x)$. Apply the chain rule to both functions of $y$, including the trigonometric derivative.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}x}\left(y^{3} + \sin{\left(y \right)}\right)&=\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}x}\left(3 x^{2} + 3\right)\\
3 y^{2}\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}+\cos{\left(y \right)}\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}&=6 x
\end{aligned}
$$

Collect the terms containing $\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}$, factor, and solve:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left(3 y^{2} + \cos{\left(y \right)}\right)\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}&=6 x\\
\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}&=\frac{6 x}{3 y^{2} + \cos{\left(y \right)}}
\end{aligned}
$$

This formula applies on real branches where $3 y^{2} + \cos{\left(y \right)}\ne 0$.

Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a function $y(x)$. The exponential chain rule gives $\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}x}(3^{y})=3^{y} \ln{\left(3 \right)}\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}x}\left(3^{y} - y\right)&=\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}x}\left(2 x^{2}\right)\\
3^{y} \ln{\left(3 \right)}\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}-1\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}&=4 x
\end{aligned}
$$

Collect the terms containing $\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}$, factor, and solve:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left(3^{y} \ln{\left(3 \right)} - 1\right)\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}&=4 x\\
\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}x}&=\frac{4 x}{3^{y} \ln{\left(3 \right)} - 1}
\end{aligned}
$$

This formula applies on real branches where $3^{y} \ln{\left(3 \right)} - 1\ne 0$.

Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. Differentiate implicitly and collect the derivative terms:

$$
\begin{aligned}
8 x + 6+\left(4 y + 2\right)\frac{dy}{dx}&=0 \\
